<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Gropius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walter Gropius was a German architect who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1919 and designed its new building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> in 1925–1926. A pioneer of modern architecture using glass curtain walls and industrial materials, he left Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1934, worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and then taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a> from 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-gropius.jpg" alt="Walter Gropius" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WalterGropius-1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Walter Adolph Georg Gropius was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 18 May 1883, the third child of Walter Adolph Gropius and Manon Auguste Pauline Scharnweber. His great-uncle Martin Gropius designed the Kunstgewerbemuseum in Berlin as a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-friedrich-schinkel">Karl Friedrich Schinkel</a>. After studying architecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and Berlin for four semesters, he joined the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-behrens">Peter Behrens</a> in 1908, where his colleagues included Ludwig Mies van der Rohe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-corbusier">Le Corbusier</a>.</p><p>In 1910 he set up a practice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> with Adolf Meyer. The glass curtain walls of their Faguswerk shoe-last factory at Alfeld-an-der-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a> became a founding monument of European modernism. In 1914 he was drafted and served on the Western front, was wounded, and received the Iron Cross twice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1919 Gropius became master of the Grand-Ducal Saxon School of Arts and Crafts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, on the recommendation of Henry van de Velde, and transformed it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a>. The faculty included Paul Klee, Johannes Itten, Josef Albers, László Moholy-Nagy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky">Wassily Kandinsky</a>. The school aimed to extend beauty and quality to every home through well-designed industrial objects.</p><p>Its first major commission, the Sommerfeld House of 1921, was designed with Meyer. Facing political and financial difficulties, Gropius and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> in 1925, where he designed the school building, the Master Houses and the Törten housing estate. He left the Bauhaus in 1928, and Hannes Meyer took over.</p><p>In 1934 Gropius left Nazi Germany with the help of Maxwell Fry, on the pretext of a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> with the Isokon group. He moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in February 1937 and taught at the Harvard Graduate School of Design, where he chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Architecture from 1938 until 1952. With Marcel Breuer he designed projects such as the Aluminum City Terrace housing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, and in 1938 he built his own house in Lincoln, Massachusetts. In 1945 he became senior partner of The Architects Collaborative, and in 1959 he won the AIA Gold Medal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gropius died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> on 5 July 1969, aged 86, after an operation in June. His widow, Ise Gropius, gave the Gropius House in Lincoln to Historic New England in 1980, and it is now a house museum. In 1996 the Bauhaus Building and the Master Houses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> were added to the list of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> district of Gropiusstadt is named after him. The Gropius-Bau in Berlin, by contrast, is named for his great-uncle Martin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walter Gropius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From June 1907 Peter Behrens’ work for the electrical company AEG showed that art and mass production could be joined on a large scale, and both <b>Walter Gropius</b> and Adolf Meyer worked for him.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1925 the Bauhaus moved from Weimar to Dessau, where <b>Walter Gropius</b> designed its new building.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-anhalt">Free State of Anhalt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1938" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Walter Gropius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5300 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1938">What was here in 1938? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
